und  recovering from a non emergency heart  procedure he barely had time to get used  to the idea of surgery before he was on  the operating table  I was waiting at home for a week I could  have been at home for longer but this  way I had it behind me  so the appointment was okay it happened  too fast is not an observation your tier  in all national healthcare systems still  mr. Conrad will have plenty of time as  an inpatient to get over the shock  Germans spend much longer recovering in  hospital than patients in the UK don't  forget a lot of patients like to be in  the hospital sometimes I have a problem  to tell them no you're ready you can go  homes at all can I please stay another 2  or 3 days it's nice here the speed of  surgery the long stays in hospital the  cutting-edge equipment thanks in part to  a historically well-funded Health System  in Germany dating back more than a  century where money is ring-fenced and  where workers have insurance payments  deducted its source  it's this man the first Chancellor of  the German Empire Otto von Bismarck  who's credited with laying the  foundations of what is the modern  European welfare state his 1883 Health  Insurance Act compelled workers to set  aside a portion of their income to  protect them from the costs of future  sickness the model persists to today and  is based on the principle of solidarity  so the young helps support the old the  better of help support the less well-off  and workers and employers now share the  costs and it works  compulsory health insurance guarantee  high-quality medical care to almost all  of Germany's 82 million citizens have  taken from the workers wages the other  half from their employer polls  consistently say most people are happy  with the service they get our strength  is that we have full coverage of some  90% of the population these people  contribution to a sickness fund  generating income isn't everything the  demographics of Germany are changing the  population is aging and the way money is  spent is now under fierce scrutiny so  our system really likes to act and for  example to operate whenever the  opportunity is there so we do have the  highest level of substitutes for needs  substitutes for hips but everything  would which is connected was long-term  care is a very weak point and we have  freely difficulties to to keep step with  the huge amount of people in need of  care and nursing tried a couple of hours  from Berlin to the rural area of temp  lien and it's easy to see the changing  face of this nation this is where German  Chancellor Angela Merkel grew up and  where her mother still lives as part of  a growing elderly population the young  are leaving gravitating to the bigger  towns and cities family support  structures are breaking down  and so in Templin nurses are taking part  in a multimillion-pound trial to take  care into the community a relatively  undeveloped area in Germany here case  manager evening Steffler assesses the  needs of a 70 year old woman being  helped to stay in her own home after  breast cancer she's got a care status  through us with it she gets an assistant  who helps her at home she helps with  home tasks hygiene helps with carrying  her shopping because she can't take it  up the stairs yeah I mean everybody  wants to stay at home and nobody wants  to go into a care home absolutely nobody  does okay I wouldn't think it is perhaps  surprising in a country which has one of  the oldest populations in the world that  long-term care for the elderly lags  behind other European nations like the  UK dr. Hans Joachim helming oversees the  trial in Templin and has a start warning  for Germany about the urgent need for  health care reform he probably wasn't  Lana but people always just thought that  things would just work themselves out  now we realize that it is almost too  late but we have to react now because  things are sharply escalating the people  are getting older and older and the  number of young people going into health  care professions is declining and the  number of young people who can pay for  the health care system is declining up  the pressures on the system are not lost  on those who could soon themselves be  more dependent on care at the Templin  seniors club there's plenty of praise  but also concern about a health service  which they've spent decades contributing  to will it deliver when they need it  most  I hope so I heard so you shouldn't  always assume the worst what do you guys  think well the health system gets better  or worse I would say I have a good  feeling about it there is a lot of  discussion about it's on the television  and there are a lot of promises being  made but often they are not kept and  that is the problem this is the problem  and the need to care for an aging  population highlights one of the  critical weaknesses in German health  a lack of nurses tens of thousands more  are needed in the German nursing council  forecast there will be a shortage of  300,000 trained professionals by 2030  unless something is done this scheme  care tiger in Berlin is trying to draw  more people into the profession by  making the job more attractive it  communicates with nurses via a  smartphone app sending them jobs close  to homes our nurse Christine she opens  the app she chooses the client we left  the office to go out and meet nurse  Christine and trainee Sebastian on their  rounds they can get two jobs on two  wheels and get to know many of their  patients well Christine worked in  hospitals for years and understands why  people are turning away from nursing  which has long been poorly paid time the  pressure the money you cannot complete  the family and the job together it's  very difficult and it's a flokati are  very different we have so much time for  the family for my daughter and for the  patients in Sebastian who's at the start  of his career is already wondering if  his insurance payments will get him the  care he needs thank you I think when I  reached the age of the people I care for  we will still have an acute personnel  problem I don't think that will change  in the next few decades it'll take more  than private local initiatives to halt  what some say is an impending nursing  crisis here could Germany look to the UK  for some answers  I spent 10 years in England in Yorkshire  I came to work for my first job at Hull  Royal Infirmary and went through GP  training and then finally worked as a GP  in three fields in Yorkshire Stuttgart  GP dr. Christian Savile scheme is a  great admirer of the NHS where he says  nurses do more take on greater  responsibility have better career  prospects in the UK minor illnesses are  being treated in the by a nurse  practitioners for example and there are  even prescribing nurses for example so  even for the prescription you don't need  necessarily the doctor in person to be  there you can delegate vaccinations so  there are a lot of interesting points we  can learn from the English system really  the ability to delegate work would take  pressure off dr. Cebulski  you like other GPS in Germany runs a  single doctor practice as a business on  top of medicine he is weighed down by  bureaucracy pursuing money from a  multitude of sickness funds who pay him  for each patient he sees and those  insurance companies are themselves now  trying to control costs setting limits  on the number of people he can have on  his books a lot of time is spent writing  the bills basically is seeing how you  get your money I found it very easy in  the NHS where you don't have to think as  much about how to build people you know  so there is much much more time spent  thinking how to get the patient well  people in Germany have the freedom to  visit any and as many GPS and  specialists as they want and so-called  doctor hopping adds to the burgeoning  costs the country's health care system  is one of the most expensive in the  world and spending is fueled by advances  in technology where Germany has been at  the forefront so this is one of our new  intensive care rooms at Charity Berlin  it is a whole new concept to t velop an  ICU environment that enables us to keep  sick patients awake but without stress  at the same time one of the country's  top teaching and research hospitals  they're taking a new approach to  intensive care  sophisticated light panels are used as  part of the healing process  synchronizing patients today and night  crucially patients are not fully sedated  we know that when we are sedating  patients those patient have a higher  risk of hospital mortality they have a  higher risk for developing acute brain  dysfunction and in the long term  cognitive and physical disabilities the  aim is to improve patient outcome but it  could also cut costs by preventing this  cognitive and physical dysfunctions we  can reduce ICU length of stay duration  for mechanical ventilation and the  hospital stay in total and that would  also reduce healthcare costs of course  if Germany is to keep investing in  research there will need to be  rationalization in the health system it  could mean painful choices closing  smaller hospitals restricting choice  finding alternatives to operations  change is needed distribution of money  that goes into the system there is not  on a fair level and it's not on a level  that is reflecting the objective demands  the Bismarck model has proved remarkably  resilient but he could never have  imagined when he set it up the workers  would survive the illnesses which  routinely killed people in the 19th  century the medical equipment would  evolve beyond scalpels and needles to  the high-tech and highly costly  equipment we see today and that so many  Germans would live to be 80 90 100 GM  anis health service is still one of the  best in the world but in 2018 it isn't  perfect  Michelle Clifford Sky News Germany  [Music]

